<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04GC">Warning <span data-tag="04GC">3.1.8.3</span>.</a> In the situation of Corollary <a data-tag="00UW" href="/tag/00UW">3.1.8.2</a>, the Kan complex $Q$ is not uniquely determined. However, the homotopy type of $Q$ depends only on $X$. If $Q'$ is another Kan complex equipped with a map $f': X \rightarrow Q'$, then we can write $f' = g \circ f$ for some map of Kan complexes $g: Q \rightarrow Q'$ (Remark <a data-tag="00UN" href="/tag/00UN">3.1.2.7</a>). If $f'$ is a weak homotopy equivalence, then $g$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence (Remark <a data-tag="00UD" href="/tag/00UD">3.1.7.16</a>) and therefore a homotopy equivalence (Proposition <a data-tag="00UB" href="/tag/00UB">3.1.7.13</a>). </p>
